net/utils: never block in _net_timedwait from interrupt context

_net_timedwait() is the common choke point for every network-stack semaphore
wait (net_sem_wait, net_sem_timedwait[_uninterruptible], and thus the TCP
connect-completion wait, the recv data wait, the write-buffer / IOB pool waits,
etc.). It unconditionally does net_breaklock() + nxsem_[tick]wait(), i.e. it
blocks -- even when called from interrupt/exception context.

oveRTOS's Linux personality dispatches guest socket syscalls inside the SVCall
handler (IPSR=11). Any of the above waits reached from there blocks, NuttX
attempts to switch away from the nested exception, and the scheduler ready-list
is corrupted -> a later context switch resumes a garbage tcb -> BusFault
(tcb=0x78, BFAR=tcb+offsetof(pid)=0xa8) that escalates to a forced HardFault.
It is intermittent because it only fires when the awaited resource is momentarily
unavailable (buffer pool / lock contended by concurrent RX on the HP work queue).

Blocking in interrupt context is never valid (NuttX already guards iob_timedalloc
this way with up_interrupt_context()). Apply the same invariant here: in
interrupt context, probe the semaphore with nxsem_trywait() and return
immediately without breaking/waiting on the network lock. The caller sees the
resource as unavailable (-EAGAIN) and the personality's coordinator retries the
op in thread context, where the wait is legal. Thread-context callers are
unaffected.

diff --git a/net/utils/net_lock.c b/net/utils/net_lock.c
index de27e3bc..bc432e82 100644
--- a/net/utils/net_lock.c
+++ b/net/utils/net_lock.c
@@ -69,6 +69,18 @@ _net_timedwait(FAR sem_t *sem, bool interruptible, unsigned int timeout)
   int          blresult;
   int          ret;
 
+  /* Never block from interrupt/exception context (e.g. oveRTOS's Linux
+   * personality dispatches guest socket syscalls inside the SVCall handler,
+   * IPSR != 0). A context switch from a nested exception corrupts the
+   * scheduler. Probe without waiting; the caller treats failure as EAGAIN and
+   * retries in thread context.
+   */
+
+  if (up_interrupt_context())
+    {
+      return nxsem_trywait(sem);
+    }
+
   /* Release the network lock, remembering my count.  net_breaklock will
    * return a negated value if the caller does not hold the network lock.
    */
